# Typo coverage

This document deliberately uses misspelled keys to pin Taliesin's schema validators.
Each unknown key below produces one click-to-source warning; rendering is unaffected.

The front matter covers three *different* mistakes on purpose, because they need three
different messages: a **typo** gets a did-you-mean (`treme`, `cach`, `max-itemz`,
`lemna`), a **retired** name gets a removal note instead of a guessed rename
(`proposition`), and a **recognized but inert** key gets neither, because there is
nothing to rename and nothing was removed (`csl:` parses, and has never had an effect).
`theorems: shared:` is the list whose *values* went unchecked until 2026-08-07: a typo
there silently drew separate counters.

::: {.callout-warnign}
This callout names a kind Taliesin does not define.
:::

::: {.scrolley}
This div names a feature class Taliesin does not define; it silently degraded before DX5.
:::

::: {.fragment}
This div names a class Taliesin RETIRED (with the slide-deck engine, 2026-08-08) rather
than one it never had, so it must draw the register's removal note instead of a rename
hint. Div classes are an open vocabulary, so without the register entry this gets silence
and the page quietly loses its layout.
:::

::: {.step lines="1|2-3"}
This scroll step's `lines=` uses the `code-line-numbers=` `|` step separator, which a
step's comma-only parser silently focuses to zero lines.
:::

::: {.input name="k"}
:::

::: {.columns}
::: {.column}
`.columns` and `.column` were withdrawn on 2026-08-02: a leftover one must say it was
REMOVED, not be answered with a did-you-mean pointing at a class that survived.
:::
:::

```{python}
#| labl: fig-demo
print("hello from a cell whose option key is misspelled")
```

